# ThreadSanitizer suppressions for `make test-tsan` (linux-tsan preset).
#
# Scope: our code is instrumented; Qt, PJSIP, and the other vcpkg deps are
# not. TSan still models their pthread-level locking, but lock-free /
# atomic-based internals of uninstrumented libraries can surface as
# false-positive reports attributed to their symbols. Suppress by library or
# top frame, narrowly, and document why — a suppression here is a hole in
# the regression gate.
#
# Syntax: https://github.com/google/sanitizers/wiki/ThreadSanitizerSuppressions
#
# Note: `make test-tsan` runs with detect_deadlocks=0. PJSIP internally
# acquires its own mutexes in both orders across different code paths
# (pj_mutex_lock <-> pj_mutex_lock cycles flagged on the very first
# makeCall), and a deadlock suppression matching pj frames would also mask
# any cycle involving CallManager's m_mutex — so lock-order discipline is
# enforced by the contract in CallManager.h + review, while TSan's data-race
# detector (the actual regression gate) stays fully active.
#
# PJSIP closes a media/RTP socket on the hangup path while its 'clock'
# media thread is mid-sendto on the same fd — internal to pjproject's media
# transport teardown; our code never touches these sockets directly.
race:pj_sock_close

# pjlib pool teardown (reset_pool / cpool_release_pool on the hangup path)
# races pj_log formatting strings out of the same arena on another thread —
# internal to pjlib's pool+logging machinery. Suppressed from both sides:
# the pool-release leaf and the logger. Our containers never pass through
# pj_log, so this can't mask CallManager-class races.
race:reset_pool
race:cpool_release_pool
race:pj_log

# pjsua updates a call's info strings (per-call pool / dialog arena) on the
# worker thread under different pj locks than pjsua_call_get_info takes to
# copy or format them out — internal pjsua locking inconsistency hit by any
# getInfo() during a state transition. Our data never flows through these pj
# string helpers, so suppressing them doesn't mask CallManager-class races.
race:pj_strncpy
race:pjsip_url_print

# pjsua destroys a call/dialog mutex on the teardown path while another
# PJSIP-API caller is acquiring it — internal pj lock-lifetime handling
# during hangup.
race:pj_mutex_destroy

# pjmedia copies a stream's RTCP statistics out (hangup-path call dump,
# getStreamStat) while the media clock thread keeps updating them — pjmedia
# does not lock stat reads against the stream thread.
race:pjmedia_stream_common_get_stat

# Qt hands cross-thread QMetaObject::invokeMethod functors through its event
# queue, which synchronizes via QBasicMutex futexes invisible to TSan in an
# uninstrumented QtCore — the functor's heap storage (operator new on the
# posting thread, executed on the receiver thread) gets flagged. Suppressing
# the posting-side helper keeps genuine races on lambda-captured data
# visible: their writer is application code, not the invokeMethod
# allocation.
race:invokeMethodCallableHelper
